/SmartGallery
temp

# AI DAM (WI-31): provisioned model weights and derived caches — never commit
.AImodels/
# Stray runtime-named dirs at repo root shadow imports as namespace packages
/open_clip/
/torch/
/transformers/
/timm/
/mobile_sam/
.ai_cache/
*.gguf
*.onnx
*.npz
.sqlite_cache/
.thumbnails_cache/
.omniquery/
.coverage
# pytest here rebuilds this cache WITHOUT its own .gitignore (measured by
# envelope: deleted, re-ran, no self-ignore came back), so trees were clean
# only by accident of already holding that file.
.pytest_cache/
# Benchmark scratch is machine-local and regenerable; the whole directory is
# ignored with NO allowlist. Evidence that shipped constants cite lives in
# benchmarks/evidence/ -- tracked wholesale. gitignore never affects tracked
# files, so the files already committed under results/ (bench manifests, the
# informational faiss/grounding/wi31 reports) stay tracked without unignore
# lines; the old hand-maintained `!` list was the defect (wrong in both
# directions: cited artifacts ignored, entries cited by nothing). A citation
# to benchmarks/results/ is a defect; cite benchmarks/evidence/.
benchmarks/results/*
# Vendored Windows GPU faiss build ships its extension module
!vendor/faiss-gpu-win64/faiss/*.pyd

# Personal launch scripts. The README has every user make these by copying
# the sample_run_* files and filling in their own paths, passwords and
# ports, and tells them to carry the same file across upgrades. One of ours
# in the repo would overwrite theirs on update, so these stay local.
run_smartgallery.bat
run_exhibition.bat
run_smartgallery.sh
run_exhibition.sh

# built database: reproducible via `python -m db.build`
db/*.db
db/*.db-wal
db/*.db-shm
db/*.backup

# npm: biome lives in node_modules; package-lock.json is committed. The
# contract generator has its own tree (frontend/codegen) because it needs a
# TypeScript the application does not; `node_modules/` matches at any depth.
node_modules/
# esbuild's output is COMMITTED, deliberately, and this is the one place
# generated code is. Running this application is `uv sync` and one command;
# it is not a Node project and nobody should need npm to look at their own
# photographs. The bundles exist because a browser cannot resolve a bare
# specifier like "openapi-fetch" -- that is the toolchain's problem, not the
# person's, so the toolchain's answer ships with the repository.
#
# `just web build` rewrites them; the gate rebuilds and fails on a diff, so a
# committed bundle can never drift from the source it was made from.
#
# The sourcemaps are NOT committed: they are three quarters of the weight and
# only a developer with the source open wants them, who can rebuild.
!sg_web/static/build/
!sg_web/static/build/*.js
sg_web/static/build/*.map

# Jinja's compiled templates (sg_web/app.py _bytecode_dir): a cache of
# this machine's Python and the templates beside it, rebuilt on demand.
sg_web/.template-cache/

# The proof machinery's local state (justfile prove / prove-push): the
# tree hash the last green full run proved, and pytest-testmon's
# per-test coverage database. Both are facts about THIS machine's runs.
.proven-tree
.testmondata
.testmondata-shm
.testmondata-wal

# Wall clock, not evidence. It is a fact about the machine that ran the
# suite, and keeping it out of compat/generated/cases.json is what lets
# `just compat attack` assert two runs produce byte-identical evidence.
compat/generated/timings.json

# The compat suite's persistent memo (compat/corpus/cache.py): decoded
# frames and our own producer's faces, namespaced by the evidence identity
# digest. Derived, machine-local, and rebuilt by running the suite.
compat/.cache/

# Per-shard wall clock, written beside each partial. Ignored for the same
# reason compat/generated/timings.json is: a fact about the machine, and
# keeping it out of the partials is what lets two runs serialise identically.
compat/generated/shards/*.timings.json

# The exclusive lock one case run holds over compat/generated/shards/
# (compat/harness/sharded.py `only_one_run`). It exists only while a run is in
# flight, and a lock left behind by a killed run is a machine-local fact.
compat/generated/shards.lock
